WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.070
Every company has documents nobody has time to read —

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.140
and a chatbot that guesses is worse than none.

3
00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:12.670
This is a search desk over my own engineering rulebook:

4
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.110
four documents,

5
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.620
over twenty thousand lines,

6
00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.590
in Russian — so I'll translate.

7
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.280
I ask how many quality checks the rulebook allows.

8
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.830
The answer comes back with an address:

9
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.240
the file and the section.

10
00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:36.770
Twenty-two checks: sixteen at every commit, six outside.

11
00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:42.140
I open that section — and it says exactly that.

12
00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.930
Now a question the documents don't cover:

13
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.290
how to run load tests.

14
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.750
Search finds nothing close enough,

15
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.720
so the AI gets no text at all —

16
00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:01.600
and it says not covered, instead of making something up.

17
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:04.950
That's the rule here:

18
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.230
an answer with an address, or no answer.

19
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.290
In six hundred and eighty answers,

20
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.010
not one broke that rule.

21
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.610
And it's measured, not assumed.

22
00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:22.290
On three hundred and fourteen questions it had never seen,

23
00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.460
the right section comes up three times out of four.

24
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.110
It all runs on a laptop,

25
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.870
so no document leaves the building.

26
00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.490
Your policies,

27
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.310
contracts or knowledge base can work the same way.
